{"agent":"architecture","findings":[{"id":"arch-001","file":"triggarr/web/validation.py","line":85,"title":"IPv4-mapped branch re-checks the address properties but skips the BLOCKED_HOSTS list, so a mapped Alibaba metadata IP passes","category":"abstraction","cwe":"CWE-918","severity":"medium","agent_confidence":70,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":{"doc":"59-CONTEXT.md","section":"D-10","quote":"Add IPv4-mapped IPv6 address checks to `validate_arr_url`. Check `addr.ipv4_mapped` on IPv6 addresses and validate the mapped address against the same blocklist. Also block multicast addresses.","confidence":0.6},"problem":"validate_arr_url blocks addresses in two ways: an exact string match against BLOCKED_HOSTS (line 77), and the ipaddress property checks (line 82). The new mapped-address branch repeats only the property checks. It never compares str(mapped) against BLOCKED_HOSTS. For example, http://[::ffff:100.100.100.200]/ gets hostname '::ffff:100.100.100.200', which does not match the string entry '100.100.100.200'. The mapped address 100.100.100.200 is not link-local, loopback, unspecified or multicast, so the URL is accepted. 169.254.169.254 is still blocked only because it also happens to be link-local. D-10 asks for the mapped address to be checked 'against the same blocklist', so this falls short of the plan.","current_code":"        if isinstance(addr, ipaddress.IPv6Address) and addr.ipv4_mapped:
